# Slice 2 — one goal drives a code repair, on the new floor.
#
# The same off-by-one guard bug engine 2 repaired, re-derived here so the result
# checks against a real prior one rather than against nothing.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHAT REPLACES "A RULE'S CONDITION IS ITS PARAMETER TYPE". That is what let
# engine 2 delete forward chaining, and survey §2 lists it as having no counterpart
# — the one item that was *a redesign, not a port*. The answer measured here is
# `+unmet($p, ...)`: a repair is proposable only once BACKWARD READING has found
# the goal unmet, so the search still discovers the order. The difference is that
# the condition is now an ordinary ANTECEDENT MEMBER rather than a type on a
# parameter, which means it is arguable and another author can write a different
# one. `experiments/restrider_repair.py` measures the control — with the `unmet`
# member removed, the repair fires on CORRECT code too.
#
# ⚠ IMAGINATION DERIVES, REALITY EXECUTES — carried over deliberately. What a case
# returns is derived from STRUCTURE by a tool; running the emitted source is a
# separate, independent gate. Engine 2 reached this from the opposite direction
# (its dispatcher refused an imagined target, so a candidate repair could never be
# evaluated by running the patched code). Here it is a choice, and the same one.
#
# ⚠⚠ REPAIR ON AN APPEND-ONLY CHAIN IS DENY-THEN-ASSERT. Nothing is mutated and
# nothing is removed, so `-operator($g, gt), +operator($g, ge)` is the substrate's
# own answer to *change this*. `Facts.of` asks `holds`, so a reader sees the
# current claim and `emit` renders the repaired code — engine 2 shipped a plan that
# "succeeded" while emitting byte-identical source, and only an independent gate
# caught it.

# Where the guard is: navigate the intaken structure to the comparison a
# function's first `if` tests. Python's vocabulary throughout — this is a reading
# of code, not a description of it.
# ⚠⚠ EVERY RULE BELOW CARRIES `no <its own conclusion>` AS A PREMISE, AND A NEW RULE
# WITHOUT ONE IS A HANG. There is no inert set in this engine: an application that
# changed nothing is offered again, so a rule that does not stop itself never stops —
# and the symptom is not an error but a run that burns its whole budget on the FIRST
# applicable rule while every later rule never fires. See `mf.py`.

rule <guard> = implies(
  { +function($f), +body($f, $bl), +stmt($bl, $i), +if_stmt($i), +condition($i, $c),
    +comparison($c), no guard($f, $c) },
  { +guard($f, $c) } )

# ⭐ The inverses the evaluator needs to walk UP. A proposition relates its members
# and nothing about it is directional, but our reader indexes by first member — so
# *which `if` is this the condition of* is an authored rule rather than a second
# index. That is the substrate's own shape: navigation is a claim, not a pointer.
rule <guard-of>  = implies(
  { +if_stmt($i), +condition($i, $c), no if_stmt_of($c, $i) },
  { +if_stmt_of($c, $i) } )
rule <in-block>  = implies(
  { +block($b), +stmt($b, $s), no block_of($s, $b) },
  { +block_of($s, $b) } )

# Ask what the function does for a case. The ANSWER is a tool's (see below), so
# what this rule writes is the request.
# ⚠ `+guard($f, $g)` is not decoration: without it this fired BEFORE <guard> had
# navigated to the comparison, the tool answered *no guard*, and nothing asked
# again — a request is a fact and a fact is not an event. That is `artefact.py`'s
# recorded ⚠⚠⚠ (*without a re-ask the signal is stale*) arriving here on the first
# run. A request must not be askable before what it needs exists.
rule <ask> = implies(
  { +function($f), +case($c), +guard($f, $g), no evaluate($f, $c) },
  { +evaluate($f, $c) } )

# ⭐ The goal. Backward reading expands it into exactly two subgoals — what the
# code does, and what was expected — and `_settle` binds `$v` from `expects`, so
# the `evaluated` subgoal is asked about the value that was WANTED.
# ⚠⚠ THE ORDER OF THESE TWO MEMBERS IS LOAD-BEARING, AND GETTING IT WRONG MAKES THE
# PLAN BLAME THE WRONG HALF. `_settle` takes the FIRST entry that satisfies a
# subgoal and nothing backtracks over the binding, so whichever member comes first
# binds `$v`. With `evaluated` first, `$v` binds to what the code DOES ('minor'),
# and the plan reports `wants` unmet — i.e. *your expectation is wrong*. Measured,
# not reasoned: that is exactly what came back.
#
# ⭐ THE RULE THIS GIVES: in a conjunction where one half is a GIVEN and the other
# is REPAIRABLE, the given must bind first. `wants` is the case we were handed and
# nothing may revise it; the code is the thing that can change. Upstream's own
# `backward.py` says the same from the other side — *which tap gets tried is
# decided by the walk, and therefore by authoring order*.
rule <checked> = implies(
  { +wants($f, $c, $v), +evaluated($f, $c, $v), no agrees($f, $c) },
  { +agrees($f, $c) } )

# ⭐⭐ THE TWO REPAIR FAMILIES. Both genuinely fix the bug, which is the point:
# "found A" must not be read as "B is wrong". ⚠ Engine 2 pinned its winner by name
# and the pin went quietly VACUOUS when upstream's tie-break flipped — so the probe
# derives the rival from the plan instead of naming it.
#
# CHANGE then OBSERVE: each denies the old claim, asserts the new one, and re-asks
# the evaluation under a fresh occasion. A repair is not done until its effect is
# observed.

# ⚠⚠ EACH REPAIR DENIES ITS OWN OCCASION, AND LEAVING THAT OUT CAUSED OVER-REPAIR.
# `unmet` is a FACT, and a fact is not an event: once backward reading has written
# it, it stays written on an append-only chain — so after `<lower>` fixed the code
# and the goal came to hold, `<relax>` was **still proposable off the same stale
# `unmet`** and fired too. The emitted result was `if age >= 17`: two independent
# fixes for one bug, still correct by luck and wrong as a repair.
#
# ⭐ It is the same shape as `artefact.py`'s ⚠⚠⚠ (*without a re-ask the signal is
# stale*) seen from the other side — there a stale signal reports work still to do;
# here it authorises work already done. `-unmet(...)` is the repair CONSUMING the
# occasion it acted on, which is upstream's own `<redo>` shape.

rule <relax> = implies(
  { +unmet($p, evaluated($f, $c, $v)), +guard($f, $g), +operator($g, gt) },
  { -unmet($p, evaluated($f, $c, $v)),
    -operator($g, gt), +operator($g, ge), +relaxed($g),
    +again(evaluate($f, $c), relaxed($g)) } )

rule <lower> = implies(
  { +unmet($p, evaluated($f, $c, $v)), +guard($f, $g), +right($g, $r),
    +literal($r, 18) },
  { -unmet($p, evaluated($f, $c, $v)),
    -literal($r, 18), +literal($r, 17), +lowered($r),
    +again(evaluate($f, $c), lowered($r)) } )
